<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Åsta Gudbrandsdatter</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Åsta Gudbrandsdóttir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Åsta Gudbrandsdatter was a Norwegian noblewoman of the late 10th and early 11th centuries, according to the sagas. She was the mother of King Olaf II Haraldsson by her first husband, Harald Grenske, and by her second husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-syr">Sigurd Syr</a>, petty king of Ringerike, the mother of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asta-gudbrandsdatter.jpg" alt="Åsta Gudbrandsdatter" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Halfdan Egedius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olav_den_helliges_saga_-_Kong_Olav_med_sine_halvbroedre_-_H._Egedius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The main source for Åsta's life is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heimskringla">Heimskringla</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>'s 13th-century collection of sagas of the Norwegian kings. She was the daughter of Gudbrand Kula and Ulfhild, and was born around 975 to 980. Snorri describes her as generous and high-minded, a keen political player and a guiding influence on her husbands and children.</p><p>In the Saga of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> she appears as the wife of Harald Grenske, ruler of Vestfold. In the summer of 994 Harald went to the Baltic and proposed to his foster-sister Sigrid, promising to abandon Åsta, who was less well born than he. Sigrid refused, reminding him that Åsta was carrying his child, and when he followed her home she had the hall where he slept set on fire after a feast; Harald was killed. Åsta, outraged by both his faithlessness and his murder, returned to her father in Oppland, where she gave birth to Olaf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Soon afterwards she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-syr">Sigurd Syr</a>, who raised Olaf in his household. When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-tryggvason">Olaf Tryggvason</a> came to Ringerike in 998 to convert its people, Sigurd, Åsta and the boy were baptised, the king standing godfather to Olaf. With Sigurd she had Guttorm, Gunnhild, Halfdan, Ingerid and Harald, the later Harald III.</p><p>In 1007 she had her steward Hrane take the twelve-year-old Olaf to sea as commander of a warship; by custom a captain of noble birth was called king, so she won him a title before he held any land. When he came home in 1014 she had her household receive him as a great king, and Sigurd wondered whether she could lead him out of the venture with the splendour she led him into it.</p><p>She backed his plan to make himself sole ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, saying she would rather he were supreme king for a short time than live and die no greater than Sigurd. After Olaf's victory over the Oppland kings in 1018 she held a great victory feast. She died around 1020 to 1030.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Åsta Gudbrandsdatter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its best-known king, Sigurd Syr, married <b>Åsta</b>, mother of Olaf Haraldsson, and supported Olaf's bid for the Norwegian throne in 1015.</blockquote><a href="/state/ringerike">Ringerike</a></li></ul></section>
